[
 {
  "id": "matras",
  "title": [
   "কার",
   "Kar",
   "Vowel signs (matras)"
  ],
  "blurb": "The signs that change a consonant's vowel: what each looks like, where it is written, and how to read it.",
  "sections": [
   {
    "id": "what",
    "title": "What a vowel sign is",
    "blocks": [
     {
      "t": "p",
      "text": "Every Bengali consonant already carries a short vowel sound, the inherent অ (like the 'o' in 'cot'). So ক is not just 'k': it is 'ko'. When a word needs a different vowel, we attach a vowel sign to the consonant. Bengali calls these signs কার (kar), so আ-কার means 'the sign for আ'. For example, ক + ◌া = কা (kaa)."
     },
     {
      "t": "note",
      "text": "People often say 'matra' for vowel signs, the way Hindi does. In Bengali the vowel signs are called কার (or স্বরচিহ্ন), and মাত্রা usually means the horizontal line on top of letters. Ask your teacher which word your class uses."
     },
     {
      "t": "ex",
      "title": "Letter + sign = a new sound",
      "items": [
       [
        "ক + া = কা",
        "k + aa = kaa",
        "ক with the আ-কার"
       ],
       [
        "ক + ি = কি",
        "k + i = ki",
        "ক with the ই-কার"
       ],
       [
        "ক + ো = কো",
        "k + o = ko",
        "ক with the ও-কার"
       ]
      ]
     }
    ]
   },
   {
    "id": "table",
    "title": "The 11 vowels and their signs",
    "blocks": [
     {
      "t": "p",
      "text": "There are 11 vowel letters. অ has no sign because it is already part of every consonant. The other ten each have a sign. The long ঈ and ঊ look different from ই and উ, but in modern Bengali they sound the same as the short ones."
     },
